 "beats": [
  {
   "type": "title",
   "speaker": "q",
   "scene": "globe",
   "text": "Did Rome really wipe out two cities in one year?"
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "a",
   "text": "It did. In 146 BC, Rome destroyed Carthage in Africa and Corinth in Greece.",
   "say": "It did. In one forty-six BC, Rome destroyed Carthage in Africa and Corinth in Greece."
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "q",
   "text": "Where was Carthage?",
   "scene": "today",
   "date": "Today",
   "place": "Tunis, Tunisia"
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "a",
   "text": "On the coast of Tunisia. Its ruins lie in a suburb of Tunis today."
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "q",
   "text": "What was it like?",
   "scene": "dido",
   "move": {
    "from": [
     0.5,
     0.5,
     1.0
    ],
    "to": [
     0.55,
     0.48,
     1.3
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "a",
   "text": "Rich. Founded by traders from Phoenicia, it ruled the sea trade of the western Mediterranean for centuries."
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "q",
   "text": "So why did Rome want it gone?",
   "scene": "sea"
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "a",
   "text": "Fear. A senator named Cato ended every vote with: Carthage must be destroyed. He even showed the Senate fresh figs from Africa, just three days' sail away."
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "q",
   "text": "Did Carthage start the war?"
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "a",
   "text": "It fought a neighbouring king without Rome's permission, which the old peace treaty banned. In 149 BC, Rome sent an army.",
   "date": "149 BC",
   "place": "Carthage",
   "say": "It fought a neighbouring king without Rome's permission, which the old peace treaty banned. In one forty-nine BC, Rome sent an army."
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "q",
   "text": "Couldn't they just give in?",
   "scene": "demands"
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "a",
   "text": "They tried. They sent three hundred children as hostages and gave up all their weapons. Then Rome told them to leave their city and move inland."
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "a",
   "text": "That, they refused."
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "q",
   "text": "With no weapons?",
   "scene": "siege"
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "a",
   "text": "They made new ones every day. Women even cut off their hair to make ropes for catapults. The siege lasted three years."
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "a",
   "text": "In 146 BC, the Romans broke in, and the fighting went on street by street for six days.",
   "date": "146 BC",
   "place": "Carthage",
   "say": "In one forty-six BC, the Romans broke in, and the fighting went on street by street for six days."
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "q",
   "text": "And then?",
   "scene": "burn",
   "move": {
    "from": [
     0.5,
     0.55,
     1.0
    ],
    "to": [
     0.5,
     0.45,
     1.25
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "a",
   "text": "The city burned. About fifty thousand survivors were sold as slaves. The Roman commander, Scipio, is said to have wept, fearing Rome might one day share the same fate."
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "q",
   "text": "Did they really salt the earth?",
   "scene": "salt"
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "a",
   "text": "That's a myth. No ancient writer mentions it. The story was invented in modern times."
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "q",
   "text": "And Corinth?",
   "scene": "corinth",
   "date": "146 BC",
   "place": "Corinth, Greece"
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "a",
   "text": "The same year, a Roman army sacked Corinth. The men were killed, women and children enslaved, and its statues shipped to Rome."
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "q",
   "text": "Why so brutal?",
   "scene": "empire"
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "a",
   "text": "As a warning. After 146 BC, no power around the Mediterranean could stand up to Rome.",
   "say": "As a warning. After one forty-six BC, no power around the Mediterranean could stand up to Rome."
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "q",
   "text": "So were they gone forever?",
   "scene": "rebuilt"
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "a",
   "text": "No. Julius Caesar planned to rebuild both. Roman Carthage grew into one of the biggest cities of the empire."
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "a",
   "text": "Next time: Julius Caesar crosses the Rubicon. Like the video and follow History Sketches so you don't miss it."
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "q",
   "text": "And tell us in the comments: was Rome right to fear Carthage?"
  },
  {
   "type": "end",
   "text": "Next: the Rubicon",
   "sub": "Follow History Sketches"
  }
 ]
}